  "query_preview": "..Utah is on the western periphery of a mean upper \nridge covering much of the central CONUS. Weak troughiness \npermeating this ridge across Arizona through Utah and into western\nColorado on Thursday will result in isolated to widely scattered \nand generally weak diurnal convection over the higher terrain of \nsouthern Utah, as moisture availability remains rather low to aid \nanything particularly substantial. Main threats from convection \nwill be locally gusty winds.\n\nOn Friday, southwesterly winds will increase ahead of an upper \ntrough grazing northern Utah Friday night. This trough will \nmaintain a threat of widely scattered convection, mainly over the \nhigher terrain and eastern valleys before a drier airmass arrives\non Saturday.\n\nFocus then shifts to an upper trough that is forecast to deepen\noff the West Coast by Sunday morning. While there is still some\nmodel spread regarding the strength of this trough, it will have\nthe potential to bring more widespread precipitation to the area.\nHowever, precipitation amounts will be dependent on how much\ntropical moisture the trough will be able to tap from tropical \nsystems Polo and Odalys in the eastern Pacific. This will also be\npartially dependent on the strength of the trough. The 19z NBM \nmean has higher QPF than the 50th percentile, which generally \nshows less than 0.1 inches in most valleys over a 24-hr period on \nSunday. Current forecast has high PoPs but maintains modest QPF \nfor this period pending better model agreement. By midweek, a weak\nridge to more of a zonal flow pattern will become established \nover the area, with should bring a return to drier conditions. \n\nTemperature-wise, expect a gradual warming trend through the \nweekend, then cooler temperatures behind the trough on Monday.\nTemperatures should then gradually climb through midweek.",
